.\" nohup.1 -- maux's nohup(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text, not copied from a historic
.\" nohup(1)), so it carries no third-party notice -- MIT like the
.\" code, same posture as env.1. Rendered at build time to
.\" build/man/NOHUP.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/NOHUP: `more /usr/man/NOHUP`.
.TH NOHUP 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
nohup \- run a utility with the hangup signal ignored
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nohup
.I utility
[
.I argument
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nohup
runs
.I utility
with the SIGHUP signal set to be ignored, then exits with its
status. On this machine nothing ever SENDS a hangup signal (no
controlling-terminal-drop concept), so the ignore step is a
real, harmless syscall rather than a fiction -- the SUBSTANCE of
.I nohup
here is the output redirection.
.PP
If standard output is a terminal, all output
.I utility
writes to its standard output is appended to
.B NOHUP.OUT
in the current directory. If that file cannot be created or
appended to, it is instead appended to
.B NOHUP.OUT
in the directory named by the
.B HOME
environment variable; if
.B HOME
is not set, or that also fails,
.I utility
is not invoked. Before either is attempted, a message naming the
destination is written to standard error. If standard error is
also a terminal, it is redirected the same way.
.PP
.I Utility
is resolved against
.B PATH
exactly as a shell resolves a bare command word (execvp), and
its
.IR argv [0]
is the word as typed, not the resolved path.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/NOHUP  this page
.br
NOHUP.OUT  the default output destination, current directory
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), env(1), timeout(1), kill(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is that of
.I utility
if it ran; 127 if it could not be found on
.B PATH
or if neither output destination could be opened; 126 if it was
found but could not be run; 2 on a usage error (no
.I utility
operand).
.SH BUGS
ProDOS has no permission bits, so
.B NOHUP.OUT
is created with whatever access CREATE's own default grants --
there is no narrower mode to request. This machine has no
hangup-signal source at all, so the ignore step can be measured
as a successful syscall but never as a behavioural difference.
